(4)~Low degeneracy (13.8\%) combined with strong LP-quality alignment ($\rho{\approx}0.5$) enables LP selection: candidates differ in F1 more often, and LP can rank them effectively. This contrasts with SciERC (17.8\% degeneracy but weak alignment $\rho{\approx}0.24$, LP $+0.42$~pp non-significant) and CoNLL (47.4\%, LP fails).

\section{Per-Entity-Type LP Selection Analysis}\label{app:per_type_lp}

Table~\ref{tab:per_type_lp} stratifies LP selection by entity type on Few-NERD, sorted by degeneracy rate.
The degeneracy-gating mechanism holds at entity-type granularity: 7 of 8 types show positive LP selection gains, and the sole negative type (Art, $-0.17$~pp) has the highest degeneracy (19.8\%).
The correlation between per-type degeneracy and oracle headroom is strong and significant (Spearman $\rho{=}{-}0.881$, $p{=}0.004$), confirming that degeneracy directly constrains the headroom available for selection.

